commands.description:
The phrase in another case.

kind: the case to give:
- "lower", "upper": the whole phrase;
- "title": every word capitalised, the rest lower; a letter after a digit or a sign starts a word too («иван-петров 5g» → «Иван-Петров 5G»);
- "sentence", "sentence_lower": the first letter of the text and of each sentence (after «.», «!», «?» and spaces) upper or lower; the other letters stay;
- "latin_title": a word that starts with a small latin letter gets it capital («iphone» → «Iphone»);
- "latin_upper": a word with a latin letter in upper case whole («wi-fi-роутер» → «WI-FI-РОУТЕР»);
- "list_title", "list_upper": the words of `words` capitalised or upper (case and ё do not count);
- "toponyms": the place names of the bundled geo list as the list spells them («нижний новгород» → «Нижний Новгород»), the longest name from each word; punctuation at the edges of a word is ignored and kept («в москве,» → «в Москве,»), and a hyphen splits a word («ростов-на-дону» → «Ростов-на-Дону»);
- "abbreviations": a word of 2 to 5 consonants, or 3 to 5 vowels, upper («hp», «ооо»), then each word of the bundled abbreviations as the list spells it («спб» → «СПб», «usb» → «USB», «СМ» → «см»).

words: the words for "list_title" and "list_upper".

The word kinds split the phrase on any whitespace and join the words by one space.

The step rewrites the working column; with --as NAME it writes the result to a new column NAME.

Example: semkit read core.csv change-case lower write out.csv

