options.description:
what a pair finds:
- "substring": every occurrence of the text, in any case and ё as е, becomes the replacement as given;
- "phrase": a phrase equal to the text (case, ё and extra spaces do not count) becomes the replacement in the case of the phrase: lower, capitalised or upper («МОСКВА» → «САНКТ-ПЕТЕРБУРГ»);
- "regex": each match of the pattern (cyrillic letters are word chars) becomes the replacement, with `\1` and `\g<name>` for the groups.

commands.description:
The pages of the column as columns: text, links, all, main, title, description, h1. One row per page.

A cell is the HTML of one page. A cell that is empty or has no tag (a plain text) gets empty columns. The columns are added right after the column of the pages.

The page must hold its text in its HTML: a page built by JavaScript has none, so give the rendered HTML. Scripts, styles, noscript, templates and hidden elements are never counted.

- text: the visible text without the text of links;
- links: the text of links;
- all: the text with the text of links;
- main: the main content, without menus, header and footer;
- title: the title of the page; description: its meta description; h1: its first H1.

drop_noindex: leave out the text of <noindex> (the tag and the <!--noindex--> comments); by default it counts.
drop_attributes: leave out the alt and title attributes; by default their text counts.

The step reads the working column of HTML and adds the columns right after it; the rows stay.

Example: semkit read --column html pages.csv parse-html write out.csv

The usual SEO metrics of each text, the same numbers each run: a metric column per metric, a row per text.

A cell is a text; an empty cell gives zeros and an empty top. The metric columns are added right after the column of the texts.

The words are those of split-text: punctuation at the edges of a word is dropped, a hyphen or an apostrophe inside a word stays («кресло-кровать» is one word), a number with inner points, commas or hyphens is one word («31.03.2026»); case and ё are folded. Stopwords are the bundled list of the n-grams. Lemmas are the words that are no stopwords. A single-letter word, an abbreviation (г, ул, руб, шт, кв, стр…) and a unit (рублей, см, мм, мл, кг) of the bundled word rules are words but no lemmas for top, nausea, ttr, mtld and zipf.
no_digits: leave out the words with a digit («2026», «15000», «A4») of every count, the words total included, as if the text did not hold them; chars and chars_no_spaces stay those of the text as given.
exclude: words to leave out the same way, as written, with case and ё folded, not by lemma («года» does not leave out «год»).

The columns, in this order:
- chars: all characters of the text; chars_no_spaces: the same without whitespace;
- words: the words of the text, stopwords, abbreviations and units included;
- unique: distinct lemmas;
- water: stopwords / words, 0..1;
- nausea_classic: the square root of the count of the most frequent lemma (the top), 2 decimals;
- nausea_academic: that count / words, 0..1;
- top: that lemma; on a tie, the first in the text;
- ttr: distinct lemmas / lemmas, 0..1;
- mtld: Measure of Textual Lexical Diversity (McCarthy and Jarvis 2010), threshold 0.72: read the lemmas forward, each time the running type/token ratio falls to 0.72 or below, count a factor and restart; the last partial run adds (1 - ttr) / (1 - 0.72); do it backward too; mtld = lemmas / mean of the two factor counts, 2 decimals; 0 for under 2 lemmas;
- zipf: R² x 100, 1 decimal, of the linear regression of log count on log rank over the lemma counts (rank 1 is the biggest count), so f(r) = C / r^s fits; 0 for under 5 distinct lemmas.

No norms and no verdicts: the caller sets them.

The step reads the working column and adds the metric columns right after it; the rows stay.

Example: semkit read core.csv text-stats write out.csv
